From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 01:29:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fpc1020: add /dev/fpc_tee, the trustlet transport
 chardev

The missing link libfprint's fpctee driver has been waiting on: a misc
chardev at /dev/fpc_tee that loads the QSEE trustlet (and the 32-bit
cmnlib it requires first) on the first open(), then carries every
ioctl(FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER) as a dumb pipe -- build the {length, phys}
envelope, call qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(), copy the TA's in-place
response back to userspace. poll()+read() of one byte exposes the same
interrupt latch the sysfs "irq" attribute already does, cleared on read
rather than only on write, matching the single-fd contract fpctee.c's
transport already expects. The sysfs attribute is untouched -- the vendor
HAL and this port's own bring-up tooling both still read it.

This driver, not qseecom_app_load.c (a throwaway module-param probe with
no open()able node), is the natural home: it already owns the interrupt,
the reset GPIO and the regulator, and unifies the two transport models
that were otherwise unreconciled -- fpc1020.c's sysfs latch and fpctee.c's
single-fd poll+read.

The ioctl struct, buffer sizes, and envelope/cmdbuf split are taken from
what libfprint's fpctee.c actually sends (userspace/libfprint-fpctee,
fp-bringup/libfprint-fpctee) and from bringup/qseecom-app-load's proven
wire format, not invented here.

Two decisions, both real and stated in the file header rather than
assumed:

- The trustlet is loaded by THIS driver, lazily on first open, because
  qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load()'s ABI never transmits the app name, so
  app_get_id() cannot recover the id afterwards (confirmed on hardware:
  -ENOENT immediately after a load that itself succeeded). Userspace has
  no channel to that id that does not go through this same kernel-only
  SCM call, so there is nothing to gain by asking it to supply one.
- INIT (group 0x0a cmd 0x00, ~761 ms, a one-time hardware bring-up step
  with no analog in fpc-tz.c's transport-independent protocol tables) is
  sent by this driver too, once, right after a successful load -- not by
  userspace, which would face the identical retention problem the app id
  already has, and not added to fpc-tz.c, which would break its one
  reason for existing (one protocol core, several trustlet generations
  as data).

Kconfig now depends on QCOM_QSEECOM (which selects QCOM_SCM, which
selects QCOM_TZMEM) for qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send() and the TZ memory
pool the ioctl path allocates from.

Verified with `porthole build --measure` (an incremental make in the
sandbox; --yes was never passed, so nothing was pushed or flashed):
compiles clean, `CC/LD/BTF [M] drivers/misc/fpc1020.ko`, no warnings
besides one pre-existing and unrelated one on a different driver. Not
flashed and not exercised against real hardware -- finger detection is
still being chased separately.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/misc/Kconfig   |   9 +-
 drivers/misc/fpc1020.c | 598 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 580 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/Kconfig b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
index 06d49e0bfe22..89c30b48091c 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
@@ -664,13 +664,16 @@ source "drivers/misc/rp1/Kconfig"
 config FPC1020
 	tristate "FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor power/reset/irq driver"
 	depends on OF && GPIOLIB
+	depends on QCOM_QSEECOM
 	help
 	  Say Y here to support the power, reset and interrupt handling for
 	  an FPC1020 or FPC1270 match-on-chip fingerprint sensor wired to a
 	  TrustZone trustlet over SPI. This driver does not talk to the
-	  sensor itself -- it owns the IO supply, the reset line and the
-	  interrupt only, and exposes the interrupt to userspace as a
-	  latched sysfs attribute.
+	  sensor itself over SPI -- it owns the IO supply, the reset line
+	  and the interrupt only -- but it does provide /dev/fpc_tee, a
+	  chardev that loads the trustlet and carries libfprint's fpctee
+	  driver's commands to it via qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(), plus a
+	  latched sysfs "irq" attribute mirroring the same interrupt.
 
 	  This driver can also be built as a module. If so, the module
 	  will be called fpc1020.
diff --git a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
index bb6c90d1bd22..c02feb73decf 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fpc1020.c
@@ -1,28 +1,80 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 /*
- * FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor -- power, reset and interrupt.
+ * FPC1020/FPC1270 fingerprint sensor -- power, reset, interrupt, and the
+ * /dev/fpc_tee chardev that carries commands to the TrustZone trustlet.
  *
- * This driver owns exactly three things: the sensor's IO supply, its
- * reset line, and its interrupt. It sends the sensor no commands at all:
- * that is the job of the TrustZone trustlet the sensor's SPI bus is
- * wired to (TrustZone owns the SPI pads; this driver's pads are the
- * reset and interrupt lines only, and neither is fenced). Matches the
- * discipline stated outright in the vendor driver this was modelled on,
- * drivers/misc/fpc1020_platform_tee.c (FPC AB, GPL-2.0): "This driver
- * will NOT send any commands to the sensor it only controls the
- * electrical parts."
+ * This driver owns the sensor's IO supply, its reset line, and its
+ * interrupt -- and, as of the /dev/fpc_tee addition below, the one thing
+ * that can actually reach TrustZone: qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send() is a
+ * kernel-exported symbol, not a syscall, so nothing in userspace could ever
+ * call it directly no matter what node existed. This driver still sends the
+ * SENSOR no commands over SPI -- it cannot, the SPI pads are fenced by a
+ * signed devcfg and are not described in this file or touched by it -- but
+ * it does now speak the QSEECom protocol to the trustlet that owns that
+ * bus, on userspace's behalf. Matches the discipline stated outright in the
+ * vendor driver this was modelled on, drivers/misc/fpc1020_platform_tee.c
+ * (FPC AB, GPL-2.0): "This driver will NOT send any commands to the sensor
+ * it only controls the electrical parts" -- the SENSOR, not the trustlet.
  *
- * The interrupt line (FP_INT_N) is active-low in name but the vendor
- * arms it IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING, once, for the device's whole lifetime --
- * not re-armed per touch -- and taimen's own shipped HAL (decompiled,
- * see the port's bring-up notes) waits on a *latched* status through a
- * sysfs "irq" attribute: reading it returns the latch, not a raw GPIO
- * level, and any write clears the latch so the next edge can set it
- * again. A stateless level mirror does not fit that protocol -- it
- * would report "already true" forever on a line that idles high and
- * the sensor's own HAL would never block. So the latch is the point of
- * this driver, not an incidental detail: it is the one thing a bare
- * GPIO read from outside the kernel cannot reproduce.
+ * The interrupt line (FP_INT_N) is active-low in name but the vendor arms
+ * it IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING, once, for the device's whole lifetime -- not
+ * re-armed per touch -- and taimen's own shipped HAL (decompiled, see the
+ * port's bring-up notes) waits on a *latched* status through a sysfs "irq"
+ * attribute: reading it returns the latch, not a raw GPIO level, and any
+ * write clears the latch so the next edge can set it again. A stateless
+ * level mirror does not fit that protocol -- it would report "already
+ * true" forever on a line that idles high and the sensor's own HAL would
+ * never block. So the latch is the point of this driver, not an incidental
+ * detail: it is the one thing a bare GPIO read from outside the kernel
+ * cannot reproduce. /dev/fpc_tee's read() exposes the SAME latch to
+ * fprintd; the sysfs attribute stays, unmodified, for the vendor HAL and
+ * this port's own bring-up tooling (tools/qfp-*) that already reads it.
+ *
+ * libfprint's fpctee driver (userspace/libfprint-fpctee, upstreamed into
+ * pmaports' libfprint fork as drivers/fpctee/) is the reference for
+ * everything below the ioctl boundary: the envelope/cmdbuf split, the
+ * buffer sizes, and the poll()+read()-one-byte event contract all come
+ * from what that code actually does, not from a spec written here first.
+ *
+ * Two decisions this file makes, both real interface choices rather than
+ * defaults:
+ *
+ * 1. WHO LOADS THE TRUSTLET. qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load()'s own ABI
+ *    (TZ_OS_APP_START_ID_PARAM_ID) never transmits the app's name -- only
+ *    the assembled image -- so app_get_id() cannot find it again by name
+ *    afterwards (confirmed on hardware: it returns -ENOENT even
+ *    immediately after a load that itself reported success). The id is
+ *    therefore a value that exists exactly once, at load time, and nowhere
+ *    else -- userspace has no channel to it that does not go through this
+ *    same kernel-only SCM call. So this driver loads cmnlib (the 32-bit
+ *    common library QSEE requires resident before any ELFCLASS32 app,
+ *    fpctzappfingerprint included) and the trustlet itself, lazily, on the
+ *    first /dev/fpc_tee open(), and keeps the returned app_id for the
+ *    lifetime of the binding. A second load attempt within the same boot
+ *    (module reload, or a second concurrent open racing the first) is
+ *    refused by TrustZone's app-manager (QSEOS_APP_START does not restart
+ *    an already-running app) -- there is no recovery for that case, by
+ *    construction: the id was never discoverable except at the moment of
+ *    that first, already-gone, load. open() fails with whatever
+ *    qcom_scm_qseecom_app_load() returns rather than pretending to find
+ *    the old id.
+ *
+ * 2. WHO SENDS INIT. Group 0x0a (SENSOR) cmd 0x00 is a one-time hardware
+ *    bring-up step (~761 ms, measured tonight) that every real capture
+ *    needs and that fpc-tz.c's transport-independent protocol core has no
+ *    concept of -- it is specific to taimen's recovered command table, not
+ *    part of the yoshino/nile/loire tables fpc-tz.c already knows. Adding
+ *    it there would break that file's whole reason for existing (one
+ *    protocol core, several trustlet generations as data). It is scoped to
+ *    LOAD, not to any individual request -- an ioctl-level "dumb pipe"
+ *    would need userspace to somehow know to send it exactly once ever,
+ *    which is the identical retention problem as (1) and has no better
+ *    answer in userspace than it does here. So this driver sends INIT
+ *    itself, once, immediately after a successful app_load(), as the last
+ *    step of the same first-open bring-up sequence that decision (1)
+ *    already owns. Every ordinary ioctl(FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER) after that
+ *    is a dumb pipe: this file does not interpret group/cmd/status,
+ *    fpc-tz.c does.
  *
  * Copyright (C) 2026 Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
  */
@@ -30,13 +82,27 @@
 #include <linux/atomic.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
+#include <linux/elf.h>
+#include <linux/firmware.h>
+#include <linux/fs.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/miscdevice.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/poll.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
+#include <linux/sizes.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/sysfs.h>
+#include <linux/uaccess.h>
+#include <linux/wait.h>
+
+#include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h>
+#include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_tzmem.h>
 
 /* hw_reset() timings, from the vendor driver, unchanged: reset ACTIVE
  * (high), a short settle, RESET (low), a longer settle, ACTIVE again.
@@ -52,6 +118,50 @@
 #define FPC1020_RESET_HIGH2_US_MIN	5000
 #define FPC1020_RESET_HIGH2_US_MAX	5100
 
+/*
+ * TrustZone wire shape, reproduced from userspace/libfprint-fpctee's
+ * fpc-tz.c and bringup/qseecom-app-load/qseecom_app_load.c (both already
+ * proven on hardware) -- do not re-derive these numbers.
+ *
+ * ENVELOPE: fpc_send_mod_cmd_t (tz_api_yoshino.h, sonyxperiadev), exactly
+ * two u32 words -- length of the SEPARATE command buffer, and that
+ * buffer's physical address -- inside a 64-byte allocation (the vendor's
+ * own buffer size for it; the extra bytes are unused padding, kept to
+ * match the proven shape rather than trim it to 8). Sent as BOTH req and
+ * rsp to qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(): the TA's top-level dispatch result
+ * lands back in the envelope's own word 0, which this driver does not
+ * otherwise inspect.
+ *
+ * CMDBUF: {group, cmd, status, ...}, rewritten in place by the TA -- what
+ * fpc-tz.c calls buf[]. Its largest user (the DB/template commands) is
+ * FPC_TZ_BUF_WORDS*4 + 256 = 320 bytes; one page is generous headroom for
+ * that plus room to grow, matching the size qseecom_app_load.c already
+ * proved this works at.
+ */
+#define FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE		64u
+#define FPC1020_TZ_CMDBUF_SIZE		PAGE_SIZE
+
+#define FPC1020_TZ_GRP_SENSOR		0x0au
+#define FPC1020_TZ_CMD_INIT		0x00u
+
+#define FPC1020_TZ_FW_DIR		"qcom/msm8998/taimen/"
+#define FPC1020_TZ_CMNLIB_NAME		"cmnlib"
+#define FPC1020_TZ_APP_NAME		"fpctzappfingerprint"
+
+/* qseecom@86600000 { reg = <0x86600000 0x2200000>; }, the vendor DT's
+ * secapp-region, verified inside mainline's existing tz_mem reservation.
+ */
+#define FPC1020_TZ_REGION_ADDR		0x86600000UL
+#define FPC1020_TZ_REGION_SIZE		0x2200000UL
+
+struct fpc1020_image {
+	struct device *dev;
+	void *virt;
+	dma_addr_t phys;
+	size_t mdt_len;
+	size_t total_len;
+};
+
 struct fpc1020 {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct gpio_desc *reset_gpio;
@@ -59,18 +169,47 @@ struct fpc1020 {
 	int irq;
 	/*
 	 * The latch: set by the ISR, cleared by any write to the "irq"
-	 * sysfs attribute. This is what userspace polls -- not
-	 * gpiod_get_value() -- see the file header.
+	 * sysfs attribute OR by a read() of /dev/fpc_tee -- see the file
+	 * header. irq_wq wakes both the sysfs poll(2) path (via
+	 * sysfs_notify(), independent of this waitqueue) and the chardev's
+	 * own poll()/read().
 	 */
 	atomic_t latched;
+	wait_queue_head_t irq_wq;
+
+	struct miscdevice miscdev;
+	/*
+	 * Serializes ALL TrustZone traffic through this device: the
+	 * one-time load+INIT bring-up AND every later ioctl xfer. Taken
+	 * for the whole bring-up sequence in fpc1020_bring_up_locked(), so
+	 * a second opener blocks behind the first rather than racing it
+	 * into a second, refused, app_load().
+	 */
+	struct mutex lock;
+	struct qcom_tzmem_pool *tzpool;
+	bool ready;
+	u32 app_id;
 };
 
+/* Userspace's ioctl struct, mirrored byte-for-byte from
+ * userspace/libfprint-fpctee/fpctee.c's `struct fpc_tee_xfer` -- keep
+ * these two definitions in sync by hand, the same discipline fpc-tz.c
+ * already asks of its own callers.
+ */
+struct fpc1020_tee_xfer {
+	__u64 buf;
+	__u32 len;
+	__u32 pad;
+};
+#define FPC1020_TEE_IOC_XFER _IOWR('F', 0x01, struct fpc1020_tee_xfer)
+
 static irqreturn_t fpc1020_irq_handler(int irq, void *data)
 {
 	struct fpc1020 *fpc1020 = data;
 
 	atomic_set(&fpc1020->latched, 1);
 	sysfs_notify(&fpc1020->dev->kobj, NULL, "irq");
+	wake_up_interruptible(&fpc1020->irq_wq);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
@@ -120,9 +259,382 @@ static void fpc1020_disable_regulator(void *data)
 	regulator_disable(data);
 }
 
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * TrustZone transport: envelope/cmdbuf construction and the one-time
+ * load+INIT bring-up. Everything here runs with fpc1020->lock held.
+ * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
+
+static void fpc1020_put32(void *buf, unsigned int word, u32 val)
+{
+	u8 *b = buf;
+
+	b[word * 4 + 0] = (u8)(val >> 0);
+	b[word * 4 + 1] = (u8)(val >> 8);
+	b[word * 4 + 2] = (u8)(val >> 16);
+	b[word * 4 + 3] = (u8)(val >> 24);
+}
+
+static u32 fpc1020_get32(const void *buf, unsigned int word)
+{
+	const u8 *b = buf;
+
+	return (u32)b[word * 4 + 0] |
+	       ((u32)b[word * 4 + 1] << 8) |
+	       ((u32)b[word * 4 + 2] << 16) |
+	       ((u32)b[word * 4 + 3] << 24);
+}
+
+/* Builds the envelope around an already-filled cmdbuf and sends it.
+ * cmdbuf is IN/OUT: on entry it holds the request at bytes [0, len); on
+ * return (transport success) it holds the TA's response in the same
+ * range. Returns qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send()'s result -- a transport
+ * error, not the TA's own status word (cmdbuf word 2, fpc-tz.c's job). */
+static int fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020, void *cmdbuf,
+				  size_t len)
+{
+	phys_addr_t cmdbuf_phys;
+	void *env;
+	int ret;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&fpc1020->lock);
+
+	cmdbuf_phys = qcom_tzmem_to_phys(cmdbuf);
+	if (cmdbuf_phys + len > SZ_4G)
+		return -EFBIG;
+
+	env = qcom_tzmem_alloc(fpc1020->tzpool, FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE,
+			       GFP_KERNEL | GFP_DMA32);
+	if (!env)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	if (qcom_tzmem_to_phys(env) + FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE > SZ_4G) {
+		qcom_tzmem_free(env);
+		return -EFBIG;
+	}
+
+	memset(env, 0, FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE);
+	fpc1020_put32(env, 0, (u32)len);
+	fpc1020_put32(env, 1, (u32)cmdbuf_phys);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_send(fpc1020->app_id, env,
+					FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE,
+					env, FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE);
+
+	qcom_tzmem_free(env);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/* Sends {group, cmd} with an otherwise-zeroed cmdbuf and no interest in
+ * the response beyond the transport result -- used only for INIT. */
+static int fpc1020_tz_send_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020, u32 group, u32 cmd)
+{
+	void *cmdbuf;
+	int ret;
+
+	cmdbuf = qcom_tzmem_alloc(fpc1020->tzpool, FPC1020_TZ_CMDBUF_SIZE,
+				  GFP_KERNEL | GFP_DMA32);
+	if (!cmdbuf)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	memset(cmdbuf, 0, FPC1020_TZ_CMDBUF_SIZE);
+	fpc1020_put32(cmdbuf, 0, group);
+	fpc1020_put32(cmdbuf, 1, cmd);
+
+	ret = fpc1020_tz_xfer_locked(fpc1020, cmdbuf, FPC1020_TZ_ENV_SIZE);
+	if (!ret) {
+		s32 status = (s32)fpc1020_get32(cmdbuf, 2);
+
+		if (status)
+			dev_warn(fpc1020->dev,
+				 "group 0x%02x cmd 0x%02x: TA status %d\n",
+				 group, cmd, status);
+	}
+
+	qcom_tzmem_free(cmdbuf);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int fpc1020_request_and_assemble(struct device *dev, const char *name,
+					struct fpc1020_image *img)
+{
+	const struct firmware *mdt = NULL;
+	const struct firmware **segs = NULL;
+	struct elf32_hdr *ehdr;
+	unsigned int phnum, i, nsegs = 0;
+	size_t total_len, off;
+	char path[64];
+	int ret;
+
+	snprintf(path, sizeof(path), FPC1020_TZ_FW_DIR "%s.mdt", name);
+	ret = request_firmware(&mdt, path, dev);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+				     "request_firmware(%s) failed\n", path);
+
+	if (mdt->size < sizeof(*ehdr)) {
+		dev_err(dev, "%s: too small to be an ELF header\n", name);
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		goto out_release_mdt;
+	}
+	ehdr = (struct elf32_hdr *)mdt->data;
+	if (ehdr->e_ident[EI_CLASS] != ELFCLASS32) {
+		dev_err(dev, "%s: ELF class %u, expected ELFCLASS32\n",
+			name, ehdr->e_ident[EI_CLASS]);
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		goto out_release_mdt;
+	}
+	phnum = ehdr->e_phnum;
+
+	segs = kcalloc(phnum, sizeof(*segs), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!segs) {
+		ret = -ENOMEM;
+		goto out_release_mdt;
+	}
+
+	total_len = mdt->size;
+	for (i = 0; i < phnum; i++) {
+		snprintf(path, sizeof(path), FPC1020_TZ_FW_DIR "%s.b%02u",
+			 name, i);
+		ret = request_firmware(&segs[i], path, dev);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(dev, "request_firmware(%s) failed: %d\n",
+				path, ret);
+			goto out_release_segs;
+		}
+		nsegs = i + 1;
+		total_len += segs[i]->size;
+	}
+
+	if (total_len > SZ_4G) {
+		dev_err(dev, "%s: assembled image (%zu bytes) will not fit below 4 GB\n",
+			name, total_len);
+		ret = -EFBIG;
+		goto out_release_segs;
+	}
+
+	img->virt = dma_alloc_coherent(dev, total_len, &img->phys, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!img->virt) {
+		ret = -ENOMEM;
+		goto out_release_segs;
+	}
+
+	memcpy(img->virt, mdt->data, mdt->size);
+	off = mdt->size;
+	for (i = 0; i < phnum; i++) {
+		memcpy(img->virt + off, segs[i]->data, segs[i]->size);
+		off += segs[i]->size;
+	}
+
+	img->dev = dev;
+	img->mdt_len = mdt->size;
+	img->total_len = total_len;
+	ret = 0;
+
+out_release_segs:
+	for (i = 0; i < nsegs; i++)
+		release_firmware(segs[i]);
+	kfree(segs);
+out_release_mdt:
+	release_firmware(mdt);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static void fpc1020_free_image(struct fpc1020_image *img)
+{
+	dma_free_coherent(img->dev, img->total_len, img->virt, img->phys);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The one-time sequence: notify TrustZone of its app-loadable region,
+ * load cmnlib (required before any ELFCLASS32 app, fpctzappfingerprint
+ * included), load the trustlet itself, then send INIT. See the file
+ * header for why both "load" and "INIT belong here" are real decisions,
+ * not defaults. Runs at most once per binding -- see fpc1020->ready.
+ */
+static int fpc1020_bring_up_locked(struct fpc1020 *fpc1020)
+{
+	struct fpc1020_image cmnlib_img = {}, fp_img = {};
+	int ret;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&fpc1020->lock);
+
+	ret = qcom_scm_qseecom_app_region_notify(FPC1020_TZ_REGION_ADDR,
+						 FPC1020_TZ_REGION_SIZE);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(fpc1020->dev, ret,
+				     "app_region_notify failed\n");
+
+	ret = fpc1020_request_and_assemble(fpc1020->dev, FPC1020_TZ_CMNLIB_NAME,
+					   &cmnlib_img);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
